  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIt will remain a dreary rest of the day. A closed surface low \npressure will continue to move across the Gulf of Maine while \ndiffluent flow aloft adds extra lift with an upper low center just \neast of the Great Lakes. Areas south of the mountains will continue \nto see mostly light rain this afternoon along with drizzle and \nfog, although briefly embedded heavier rain rates are possible. \nGoing into this evening and tonight, expect rain to also fill \nin more farther north and west across the interior with the \nupper low approaching and moving across New England. Hi-res \nguidance is in fairly good agreement with additional rain of \n0.25-0.50\" through Sunday morning, but there are indications of\na pivot point setting up somewhere this evening or tonight to \nproduce a period of slower-moving, persistent higher rain rates.\nAlthough not certain, guidance is pointing toward western NH, \nand if to occur, localized amounts of 1.00-1.50\" are possible. \nHowever, with the airmass not as juicy as yesterday and with how\ndry it has been lately and interior areas not picking up much \nrain over the past 24 hours, am not anticipating any issues. As \ncolder air works in late, expect snow to start mixing in and \naround the mountains with light accumulations possible in the \nhigher peaks. Rain should start to taper off across southern NH \nlate and toward daybreak, and as it does so, northwest winds \nlook to become breezy with wind gusts up to 25 mph. \n\nRain will continue to taper off south of the mountains from west to \neast Sunday morning and afternoon with increased mixing \nresulting in breezy northwest winds. Based on forecast soundings\ngusts of 25 to 35 mph look reasonable, but the top of the mixed\nlayer suggests a few could approach 40 mph. Skies will be \nmostly cloudy in the morning, but drier air should bring some \npeaks of sunshine going into the afternoon hours. Highs will \nagain be cooler ranging from the upper 40s to mid 50s south of \nthe mountains. The mountains and areas to the northwest will \nremain mostly cloudy and cooler with upslope flow and ongoing \nchance for rain or snow showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nMean troughing dominates the 500 mb pattern from the period \nfollowing this current system to the middle of this coming week.\nThe result is going to be persistent northwesterly flow with \nseveral shortwaves providing the forcing for upslope rain and \nsnow showers in the mountains. Looking at Froude Numbers from \nthe current GFS it looks like flow is going to be mostly \nsubcritical with precipitation confined to the mountains, but it\nis too early to completely rule it out downwind. Daytime \ntemperatures during this time are going to be in the 50s south \nof the mountains and 40s to the north, so only the higher peaks \nof the White Mountains are likely to see snow. Nighttime \ntemperatures in the low to mid-30s may facilitate some flakes \ndown to the northern valley floors, but it isn't expected to \namount to much.\n\nWe see even cooler temperatures later in the week into the \nweekend as we get a brief extension of the polar jet overhead \nmaking for daytime temperatures more in the upper 30s and 40s. \nDeterministic global models have been consistent with bringing a\ndisturbance into the area during this time as well so I think \nit is fair to say it is a window to watch, but ensemble support \nfor any other aspect is low confidence at this time range. Let's\nsee a few more days of model runs before drawing any \nconclusions.",
